  <Placemark>
    <name>Squamish Trail Network (British Columbia) [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Granite slabs, rainforest roots and modern Canadian flow</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>More than 300 km of riding spread across Diamond Head, Alice Lake, Valleycliffe and other zones between Vancouver and Whistler. Squamish ranges from approachable flow such as Half Nelson to steep rock slabs, wet roots and highly technical black and double-black descents. It is a foundational destination for modern enduro and freeride trail culture rather than a single bike park.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no unified trail fee. Most riding is pedal-accessed on forest roads and climbing trails; commercial guiding or vehicle support can be arranged for selected rides. Donate to SORCA and use official trailheads rather than residential shortcuts.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Open from 1 April; closed from 1 December (typical lower-elevation indication).<br/>Daily opening hours: daylight only<br/><small>Some low trails ride through winter, while snow persists higher. Check closures, wildlife notices, logging operations and rain-related trail guidance.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Vancouver</td><td>Car</td><td>~1h10</td></tr><tr><td>Whistler</td><td>Car</td><td>~50 min</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.exploresquamish.com/things-to-do/activities-adventures/mountain-biking/">Tourism Squamish</a> and <a href="https://www.sorca.ca/about-our-trails">Squamish Off-Road Cycling Association</a>. Check current trail reports and support local maintenance.</i>]]></description>
