  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Kangerlussuaq Arctic MTB (Greenland) [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Hardcore tundra riding from town toward the ice sheet</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Greenland's longest connected road and surrounding rough tracks make Kangerlussuaq its most convenient mountain-bike base. The signature objective runs roughly 25 km from town toward Russell Glacier or Point 660 at the ice-sheet edge over rocky, sandy and rolling glacial terrain. This is remote adventure riding rather than gravity laps, with musk oxen and reindeer possible along the route.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. Ride out-and-back, rent locally or arrange a guide and vehicle support. Distances, loose surfaces, wind and a complete lack of services make even the road ride a substantial backcountry undertaking.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; Carry repair equipment, emergency layers, food and communication suitable for Arctic wilderness. Keep a large distance from wildlife and never approach glacier fronts or the ice sheet without expert guidance.</p>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Open from 15 June; closed from 15 September (conservative summer indication).<br/>Daily opening hours: daylight only<br/><small>There is no formal operating season. Snowmelt, dust, wind and rapidly changing cold weather determine whether the route is practical.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Kangerlussuaq Airport</td><td>Bike</td><td>Immediate</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Source: <a href="https://visitgreenland.com/activities/biking/">Visit Greenland's official Kangerlussuaq biking guide</a>. Confirm rental, road condition and safe turnaround plans locally.</i>]]></description>
